{"context":"The delivery lifecycle standard is the distributable unit of framework lifecycle knowledge (Transition Plan §5.3): a standalone executable carrying seven parts — Manifest, Lifecycle Definition, Verification, Templates, Compatibility, Documentation, Tests (§5.4) — validated against the delivery lifecycle specification and distributed through the registry. ADR-021 defines what a standard *is*; this ADR governs its lifecycle: how a standard becomes publishable, who owns it, how it is deprecated, and what bar applies to every standard equally. Source: ADR-027-delivery-lifecycle-standard-governance.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"A standard passes through five governed states (§5.7):\n\n```text\nProposed → Draft → Published → Deprecated → Retired\n```\n\n- **Proposed / Draft:** authoring and review; not discoverable for production use.\n- **Published:** discoverable and installable, validated against the declared contract version.\n- **Deprecated:** installable with warning; no updates; removal date announced.\n- **Retired:** removed from the registry with a documented migration path.\n\nPublication is accepted only when all of the following hold:\n\n1. **Structure** — all seven parts of the standard structure are present (§5.4).\n2. **Conformance** — contract conformance is validated against the declared contract version at registry acceptance.\n3. **Tests** — the standard's own tests pass, validated by the registry (registry-va","consequences":"**Positive:** a predictable standard lifecycle for adopters; one quality bar across the ecosystem; Core never becomes the bottleneck for standard fixes; deprecation is observable and scheduled, never silent.\n\n**Negative:** maintainers carry a governance burden (structure, tests, conformance, maintainership); the registry must enforce the acceptance criteria mechanically (ADR-023); Retired standards require migration paths that must remain maintained.","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Core-owned standards.** Rejected: framework knowledge belongs outside the Core (manifesto §3.5); Core ownership would make every standard fix a Core release, recreating the v1.x coupling this plan removes.\n- **Core patches non-conforming standards.** Rejected: it blurs ownership and incentivizes low-quality submissions; registry rejection keeps the boundary enforced (§5.5).\n- **Unbounded states (no Deprecated/Retired).** Rejected: silent or indefinite availability contradicts the"}